    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Cough for five days after a cold. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Robeson called this morning and was seen the same day. A week ago he had a sore throat and a runny nose, and for five days he has had a cough, worse at night, with a little clear sputum. He has had no fever, breathlessness, chest pain or blood in the sputum. The cough is keeping him awake and he wants to protect his voice. He bought a nighttime cold medicine but did not take it after reading that it contained dextromethorphan and remembering the warning about venlafaxine. He takes venlafaxine 225 mg each morning, three weeks at the full dose, with mild sweating and no other side effects. His mood is a little better than in the spring; he goes to his therapy sessions each week and walks most mornings. He has had no thoughts of suicide or of being better off dead. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Cough for five days after a cold a week ago</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Cough with a little clear sputum, worse at night</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Disturbing his sleep; no breathlessness</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Venlafaxine 225 mg daily; has avoided a dextromethorphan cold remedy</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No fever, chest pain or blood in the sputum</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
