<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute bronchitis (J20.9)</content>. Acute bronchitis after a viral cold. Pulse under 100, respiratory rate under 24, temperature under 38 °C and a normal chest examination make pneumonia unlikely, so no chest X-ray or antibiotic is needed; the cough may last two to three weeks. He was right to avoid the dextromethorphan remedy: with venlafaxine it can cause serotonin syndrome. Decongestants are also avoided because venlafaxine can raise blood pressure. [S]<list><item>Honey in warm drinks, steam and fluids; rest the voice.</item><item>Plain guaifenesin if the sputum is thick; read labels for dextromethorphan, pseudoephedrine and phenylephrine.</item><item>Return for fever, breathlessness, chest pain, blood in the sputum, or a cough lasting more than three weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Recurrent major depression on venlafaxine (F33.2)</content>. Three weeks at 225 mg. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or of being better off dead; medicines kept in the locked box; the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline is in his phone. Blood pressure 132/82. [S]<list><item>Continue venlafaxine 225 mg and weekly behavioral activation.</item><item>Review in four weeks as booked.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Appropriate Antibiotic Use for Acute Respiratory Tract Infection in Adults: Advice for High-Value Care From the American College of Physicians and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2016)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Twenty-four-week review in four weeks as booked. Return sooner for fever or breathlessness. Call or text 988 at any time in a crisis. Next scheduled visit 2026-08-24: twenty-four-week review: partial response to venlafaxine; treatment-resistant depression; aripiprazole augmentation and an informed consent discussion about ECT. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>
